# =====================================================================================================================================================
# backend/.env.example   -   the PLATFORM API (one codebase, TWO services: the customer API and the admin API)
# Written for W26-R52, restructured into blocks in W26-R54 (2026-11-03). Every name below is read by backend/app/shared/config.py (read it for the exact rules).
# Full reference: docs/setup/13-environment-variables.md.
# =====================================================================================================================================================
#
# HOW THIS FILE IS BUILT: IN BLOCKS
#   A BLOCK is a group of lines that only work TOGETHER (for example PROVISIONER=neon and its NEON_API_KEY). Every block starts with a line  ## BLOCK n: name  and ends at the
#   next one. You switch a feature on or off by uncommenting or commenting THE WHOLE BLOCK (every line of it), never half of one. Where you must choose ONE of several ways
#   (the provisioner, the sign-in provider, the billing provider) the ways are sub-blocks A, B, C: leave exactly one uncommented.
#   Lines that start with  #  followed by a NAME= are switched off. Lines of explanation never contain a NAME= at the start.
#
# HOW TO USE
#   1. Make the file for your environment by copying this one (all three are git-ignored, none is ever committed):
#        .env.local        your machine. Read by dev.ps1, dev-service.ps1 and every flask command, wherever you start them.
#        .env.staging      a notebook for the Render STAGING services, and the file you point to when you run a command from this machine against staging.
#        .env.production   the same for production.
#      Render never sees these files (they are not in git). On Render you paste the lines into each service's Environment tab, one service at a time.
#   2. For each block read its  WHERE  line, then keep it (uncommented, every line filled in) or comment the whole block. Replace every <placeholder>.
#      A line that still contains < > is a mistake. The app stops or fails loudly; it never guesses (except where it says "dev key").
#   3. Write NAME=value with no spaces around the = and no comment after the value (a comment goes on its own line above). No quotes needed.
#   4. Run a command from this machine against another file (PowerShell, from backend\):
#        $env:ENV_FILE = '.env.staging'
#        .venv\Scripts\flask.exe --app app.main:create_app db current
#        Remove-Item Env:ENV_FILE
#      ENV_FILE is a variable of the process, never a line inside a file. A value already in the shell or in Render's dashboard always wins over the file
#      (a leftover  $env:DATABASE_URL  in a window once made a server use the wrong database: close the window when you are done).
#   5. Restart the backend after any change. It never reloads by itself.
#   6. Secrets (marked SECRET): never in git, never in chat, never in a screenshot. Keep them in the password manager.
#
# THE TWO SERVICES (the same folder, started with different settings). Tags on a block or a line:
#   [C]  the customer API only      [A]  the admin API only      [C+A]  both (the same value unless the text says otherwise)
#   Blocks 3 and 4 are the two services: ONE file holds both sets as a notebook, but each Render service gets only ITS block plus the [C+A] blocks.
#   In production the customer service REFUSES to start if it holds STAFF_JWT_SECRET, STAFF_FERNET_KEY, SUPPORT_SESSION_KEY or RENDER_API_KEY. Staging does not check
#   this (ENV=local): you keep them off the customer service by hand.
#
# WHERE:  L = .env.local (your machine)   S = .env.staging (Render staging)   P = .env.production (Render production, a later pass)
#         req = required   opt = optional   no = must NOT be set there
#
# TWO MAKERS YOU WILL NEED (run in your own PowerShell, copy the line it prints into the password manager, never into chat):
#   A random secret (40 random bytes as text, no symbols; about 53 characters):
#     $b = New-Object byte[] 40; [Security.Cryptography.RandomNumberGenerator]::Create().GetBytes($b); ([Convert]::ToBase64String($b) -replace '[+/=]','')
#   A Fernet key (44 characters ending in =), from the backend folder:
#     .venv\Scripts\python.exe -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 1: BASICS   [C+A]   WHERE: L=req   S=req   P=req
# =====================================================================================================================================================

# ENV: local | test | production. NEVER test (it switches Flask's TESTING on).
# Why staging says "local": production demands Neon Auth, Neon storage, Neon squares and Stripe, which have not been run for the platform yet (guide 41, "The decisions
# already taken"). The consequence: with ENV=local the app falls back to a built-in DEV KEY for any secret left empty. So in staging every secret must be set by hand.
ENV=local
# LOG_LEVEL: DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR. Logs are JSON with a request id and never hold personal data.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# DATABASE_URL (SECRET): the platform database (guide 03). postgresql:// is accepted; the psycopg driver is added for you. A password with @ / ? # % must be percent-encoded.
#   L: the local Postgres below (role rs_admin, database rootsquares_platform), or your local Supabase project's pooler address (port 6543).
#   S: the address of the database ROLE of the service (guide 36): the customer API takes the customer_app address, the admin API the admin_app address. Two different
#      values, each pasted into its own Render service. In THIS file keep only the OWNER address, and only while you run migrations from this machine (guide 41 Step 1).
#      Never put the owner address on a Render service.
#   P: the same pattern on the production database (Neon, guide 12).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://rs_admin:rootsquares_local_dev@localhost:5432/rootsquares_platform
# FERNET_KEY (SECRET): encrypts every square's database address and keys inside the platform database. THE SAME VALUE on the customer API, the admin API AND the square
# server (square/backend). NEVER CHANGE IT once a square exists (every stored key becomes unreadable). Back it up in a second place. Make it: the Fernet maker.
# L: leave it out (the dev key is used).   S, P: required.
FERNET_KEY=<make with the Fernet maker, keep a backup>
# SQUARE_DOMAIN_SUFFIX: a square's default address is https://<slug>.<this>. Default rootsquares.com. S, P: required (set it explicitly).
SQUARE_DOMAIN_SUFFIX=rootsquares.com


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 2: SIGN-IN PROVIDER OF THE PLATFORM   [C+A]   WHERE: L=req   S=req   P=req      Choose ONE sub-block. Both lines go together.
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# The frontend's VITE_AUTH_PROVIDER and VITE_AUTH_URL (frontend/platform) must be the same two values.

## 2A: Supabase (local and staging). S: the NEW staging project from guide 41 Step 1, never your local test project.
AUTH_PROVIDER=supabase
AUTH_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# AUTH_ADMIN_KEY (SECRET, optional) [C]: the sign-in project's service key (Project Settings > API Keys > service_role or sb_secret_...). Used only to delete a person's
# sign-in account when they delete their own account. Without it their platform data is still deleted and they are told the sign-in account remains.
# AUTH_ADMIN_KEY=<service key>

## 2B: Neon Auth (production only; the platform's own login on Neon Auth has never been run). Production refuses anything but neon.
# AUTH_PROVIDER=neon
# AUTH_URL=<the Neon Auth URL>


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 3: STORAGE (the platform's own files)   [C+A]   WHERE: L=req (empty endpoint = an in-memory fake that saves nothing)   S=req   P=req (production refuses the fake)
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# S3-compatible (guide 05). Supabase: Project Settings > Storage > S3 connection: the endpoint, and "New access key" (a pair: key id 32 characters, secret 64 characters,
# shown once; made in the dashboard only). Production: Neon Object Storage. Make the two buckets first (Storage > New bucket): the public one "public", the private not.
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL MUST END WITH THE PUBLIC BUCKET NAME.
STORAGE_S3_ENDPOINT_URL=https://<project-ref>.storage.supabase.co/storage/v1/s3
STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY=<s3 access key id, 32 characters>
STORAGE_S3_SECRET_KEY=<s3 secret access key, 64 characters>
STORAGE_S3_REGION=auto
STORAGE_PUBLIC_BUCKET=rootsquares-public
STORAGE_PRIVATE_BUCKET=rootsquares-private
STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/rootsquares-public


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 4: PROVISIONING OF NEW SQUARES   [C+A]   WHERE: L=req   S=req   P=req (neon)      Choose ONE sub-block A, B or C and comment the other two.
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# How a NEW square gets its own database, sign-in and storage. Every new square creates real projects at the provider; delete a test square on the platform to delete its project.

## 4A: Neon (staging default, proven in wave 17; production requires it). Needs the key: Neon console > profile menu > Settings > API keys > Create new (guide 41,
## "Make the Neon API key"). Shown ONCE: copy it into the password manager at once. A personal or an organization key works; a project-scoped key cannot create projects.
PROVISIONER=neon
NEON_API_KEY=<neon api key>
# NEON_REGION=aws-eu-central-1

## 4B: Supabase (a real Supabase project per square through the Management API; the free plan allows very few; guide 06). One token = one account.
# PROVISIONER=supabase
# SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=<sbp_... personal access token: dashboard > Account > Access Tokens>
# SUPABASE_ORG_SLUG=<the part of the dashboard address after /org/>
# SUPABASE_REGION=eu-west-1

## 4C: local (a database inside the local Postgres; offline; DATABASE_URL must be the local Postgres)
# PROVISIONER=local

# Optional with any of the three: a command run for every new square database. LEAVE IT OUT: empty means the square app's own schema command (square_db.py upgrade-env).
# SQUARE_TEMPLATE_CMD=


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 5: THE SQUARE SERVER LINK   [C+A]   WHERE: L=opt   S=req   P=req      Both lines go together.
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# Where the shared square server (square/backend) answers, and the secret both sides share (header X-Internal-Key). Lets the owner's Modules screen ask what a module holds.
#   SQUARE_SERVER_URL   L: http://localhost:5002      S, P: https://<the square server's onrender.com address>, known only after guide 41 Step 6 (leave the block out until then,
#                       then add it and redeploy this service).
#   SQUARE_INTERNAL_KEY (SECRET) MUST EQUAL the square server's PLATFORM_INTERNAL_KEY. Make it: the random maker, 40 or more characters (the maker gives about 53).
SQUARE_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:5002
SQUARE_INTERNAL_KEY=<random 40+ characters, equal to PLATFORM_INTERNAL_KEY of square/backend>


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 6: THE CUSTOMER SERVICE   [C]   WHERE: L=opt (leave the block out: API_SURFACE=all)   S=req on the customer service   P=req on the customer service
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# API_SURFACE: customer = the customer API (and the public square data). THE ONE LINE that makes the two services different. all = both (development and tests only;
# production REFUSES it). L: leave this block out.
API_SURFACE=customer
# JWT_SECRET (SECRET): signs the customers' password-login cookies; 32+ characters in production. Different from STAFF_JWT_SECRET. Make it: the random maker.
# Guide 41 Step 4 sets the same value on the admin API in staging (not needed there in production).
JWT_SECRET=<random 32+ characters>
# CORS_ORIGINS: the browser addresses that may call the customer API with cookies. Explicit http(s) origins, comma separated, NEVER *. NEVER the admin address (the app
# refuses an origin that appears in both lists). Empty with ENV=local means http://localhost:5273.   S: https://rootsquares.com,https://www.rootsquares.com
CORS_ORIGINS=https://rootsquares.com,https://www.rootsquares.com
# PLATFORM_FRONTEND_URL: the customer app's public address: where Stripe returns the owner and what the links in e-mails point to.
#   L: http://localhost:5273 (also the default)      S: https://rootsquares.com      P: the production address
PLATFORM_FRONTEND_URL=https://rootsquares.com


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 7: THE ADMIN SERVICE   [A]   WHERE: L=opt (leave the block out)   S=req on the admin service   P=req on the admin service
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# The staff API. MUST NOT be set on the customer service (production refuses to start). To use this block for the admin service, comment BLOCK 6 and uncomment this one.
# API_SURFACE: admin = the staff API only.
# API_SURFACE=admin
# ADMIN_ORIGINS: the address of the admin application, the only page that may call /api/admin/v1. Exactly one origin per site, no path, never a customer origin. The admin site
# and the admin API must share one parent domain (so the staff login cookie is kept). Empty with ENV=local means http://localhost:5275 and the 127.0.0.1 form.
# ADMIN_ORIGINS=https://admin.rootsquares.com
# STAFF_JWT_SECRET (SECRET): signs the staff cookie rs_admin; 32+ characters in production; different from JWT_SECRET. Make it: the random maker.
# STAFF_JWT_SECRET=<random 32+ characters>
# STAFF_FERNET_KEY (SECRET): encrypts the staff authenticator secrets; different from FERNET_KEY. Changing it later means staff-reset-2fa for every staff member. Make it: the Fernet maker.
# STAFF_FERNET_KEY=<make with the Fernet maker>
# SUPPORT_SESSION_KEY (SECRET): support access (ADR 089): the admin API signs a support session with it and every square server verifies it with the SAME value
# (square/backend has the same name). 32+ characters, different from JWT_SECRET and STAFF_JWT_SECRET. Make it: the random maker.
# SUPPORT_SESSION_KEY=<random 32+ characters, equal to square/backend>


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 8: RUNNING ON RENDER   [C+A]   WHERE: L=no   S=req   P=req (COOKIE_SECURE, PROXY_HOPS)      The three lines go together.
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# COOKIE_SECURE: marks the login cookies "Secure" (sent only over https). Render is https. Production refuses false. L: leave it out (false).
COOKIE_SECURE=true
# PROXY_HOPS: how many proxies stand in front of the service. Render: 1. The site gate reads the caller's address from X-Forwarded-For with it. L: leave it out (0).
PROXY_HOPS=1
# ENV_LABEL: the word on the strip above every page: Staging. DISPLAY ONLY, nothing behaves differently because of it. P: leave it out (no strip). Empty with ENV=local = Local.
ENV_LABEL=Staging


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 9: THE SITE GATE   [C+A]   WHERE: L=no   S=req   P=no (a public site has no gate)      Both lines go together.
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# The staging passcode (ADR 099, guide 42): every request without the cookie it earns is refused. SITE_GATE_PASSWORD (SECRET): 16 or more characters, THE SAME VALUE on the
# customer API, the admin API and the square server. Changing it ends every earlier pass. Make it: the random maker. SITE_GATE_HOURS: how long a pass lasts (default 168).
SITE_GATE_PASSWORD=<random 24+ characters, the same on three servers>
# SITE_GATE_HOURS=168


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 10: PLATFORM E-MAIL   [C+A]   WHERE: L=opt   S=opt   P=opt      Comment the whole block for "send nothing" (EMAIL_PROVIDER defaults to none).
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# Guide 25. resend needs RESEND_API_KEY and EMAIL_FROM or the app will not start.
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# RESEND_API_KEY=<a Resend key limited to sending (SECRET)>
# EMAIL_FROM=rootsquares <hello@your-verified-domain>
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=hello@your-main-domain
# STAFF_NOTICE_EMAIL: where the platform e-mails its own staff when an owner asks for an own sending domain or for IT help. Empty = no notice.
# STAFF_NOTICE_EMAIL=hello@rootsquares.com

## BLOCK 10b: a Grove square's own sending domain (guide 25 section 11)   [C+A]   needs BLOCK 10 above. The three lines go together.
# RESEND_DOMAINS_API_KEY (SECRET): a FULL ACCESS Resend key, a different key from RESEND_API_KEY. Customer API and admin API, never the square server. Empty = owners can ask, nothing is set up.
# RESEND_DOMAINS_REGION: region of new domains; keep it equal to the region of the verified sending domain. It cannot be changed later.
# MAIL_DOMAIN_AUTO_APPROVE: true (default) = an owner's request is set up at once; false = staff approve each request in the admin application.
# RESEND_DOMAINS_API_KEY=<resend full access key>
# RESEND_DOMAINS_REGION=eu-west-1
# MAIL_DOMAIN_AUTO_APPROVE=true


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 11: GOOGLE SIGN-IN FOR SQUARES   [C+A]   WHERE: L=opt   S=opt   P=opt      Both lines go together (guide 09).
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# The Google OAuth client squares use (Google Cloud Console > Credentials > Web application). Empty with a Neon square = Neon's shared development credentials. With the client
# set, add each square's callback address to the client, then run: flask --app app.main:create_app enable-google <slug>
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=<client id>
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=<client secret (SECRET)>


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 12: BILLING   [C+A]   WHERE: L=opt   S=opt   P=req (stripe)      Choose ONE sub-block. Guides 22, 24; docs/reference/billing-and-payments.md.
# =====================================================================================================================================================

## 12A: fake (the default; L and S): a test checkout page, no money moves. Nothing else is needed. Leave this sub-block and 12B commented to get it.
# BILLING_PROVIDER=fake

## 12B: Stripe (P: production REQUIRES it). All three lines go together; the two keys are required or the backend will not start.
# BILLING_PROVIDER=stripe
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=<sk_test_... in the sandbox, sk_live_... live; never the publishable key (SECRET)>
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<whsec_... the signing secret of the webhook endpoint; locally the one `stripe listen` prints (SECRET)>

## Billing details (optional, with either sub-block; the value shown is the default)
# BILLING_ENFORCE=false           false = waking a sleeping square is free; true = it needs a paid period, and the trial-end job sleeps unpaid squares.
# BILLING_TRIAL_DAYS=7            free Live days of a new square
# BILLING_GRACE_DAYS=7            days a lapsed paid period stays live
# BILLING_REFUND_DAYS=14          how long after the first payment a platform admin may refund it
# BILLING_TRIAL_REMINDER_HOURS=24 the trial reminder goes out when this many hours or fewer are left
# BILLING_TAX_RATES=NO:25         COUNTRY:PERCENT pairs whose tax is inside the listed price
# STRIPE_TAX_CODE=txcd_10103001   the Stripe product tax code sent with every line
# STRIPE_TEST_CLOCK_ID=           test keys only (refused with a live key); leave empty otherwise


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 13: THE OWN WEBSITE DOMAIN (Render API)   [A]   WHERE: L=no   S=opt   P=opt      The two RENDER lines go together (guide 40).
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# With both set, staff's Activate adds the domain on Render and Remove deletes it. Either empty = staff add it on Render by hand. RENDER_API_KEY (SECRET) is a Render account
# API key (Account Settings > API Keys): it reaches your WHOLE Render account, so it lives on the admin API only (the customer API refuses it in production).
# RENDER_SITE_SERVICE_ID is the id srv-... in the address of the square SITE service's page (guide 41 Step 7).
# RENDER_API_KEY=<render api key>
# RENDER_SITE_SERVICE_ID=<srv-...>
# CUSTOM_DOMAIN_TARGET [C+A]: the one name an owner points a CNAME at (ADR 098). Default squares.rootsquares.com. Change only if you use another name.
# CUSTOM_DOMAIN_TARGET=squares.rootsquares.com


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 14: LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY   [C+A]   WHERE: L=opt   S=no   P=no
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL / SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD: the first staff account, created by dev.ps1 on every start (flask seed-staff). Password at least 12 characters. Empty email = skipped.
# On Render make the first staff account in a shell instead: flask --app app.main:create_app staff-create you@example.com --name "Your name" (guide 34).
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-please-123
# STAFF_SECOND_STEP_REQUIRED: true (the default) = the staff sign-in asks for the authenticator code. false = the password alone signs in, FOR LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY (every
# skipped step is audited). Production REFUSES false. Never set it in staging.
# STAFF_SECOND_STEP_REQUIRED=false


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 15: SQUARE LIFE AND SLEEP (all optional; the value shown is the default)   [C+A]   WHERE: L=opt   S=opt   P=opt
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# PROVIDER_SLEEP: false = Keep does not pause anything at the provider; true = Keep really pauses the square's project and restores it on wake (guide 22).
# EVENT_PASS_*: the Event pass life (guide 23). EVENT_PASS_PURGE and SLEEP_ARCHIVE_PURGE: false = the daily job only says what it would delete. Keep false until you have watched the dry runs.
# SQUARE_EXTRA_REDIRECT_URLS: extra sign-in return addresses every NEW square accepts, comma separated, {slug} is replaced. Empty with ENV=local = the localhost addresses; empty in
# production = none. Apply to an existing square: flask --app app.main:create_app sync-square-auth <slug>
# PROVIDER_SLEEP=false
# EVENT_PASS_LIVE_DAYS=45
# EVENT_PASS_ARCHIVE_DAYS=45
# EVENT_PASS_SOFT_DELETE_DAYS=14
# EVENT_PASS_PURGE=false
# SLEEP_ARCHIVE_DAYS=360
# SLEEP_ARCHIVE_PURGE=false
# SQUARE_EXTRA_REDIRECT_URLS=https://{slug}.rootsquares.com/**


# =====================================================================================================================================================
## BLOCK 16: SESSIONS AND LIMITS (all optional; defaults are fine)   [C+A]
# =====================================================================================================================================================
# ACCESS_TTL_MINUTES=15            life of the access cookie
# REFRESH_TTL_DAYS=14              life of the refresh cookie
# LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT=5               password-login attempts per address and e-mail ...
# LOGIN_RATE_WINDOW_SECONDS=60     ... per this many seconds
# STAFF_IDLE_MINUTES=30            a staff login ends after this long without a request
# STAFF_SESSION_HOURS=8            ... and in any case after this long
# SQUARE_AUTH_CACHE_SECONDS=600    how long the square token verifier keeps a square's decrypted keys in memory

# --- Leave unset -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENABLED_THEME_IDS   the themes owners may choose. The built-in list in config.py is kept equal to the frontend's list by a test; if you ever set this, the two must still be equal.
#
# Not variables of this file: ENV_FILE (see HOW TO USE, step 4), PG_BIN (dev.ps1 only: the folder of psql, default C:\Program Files\PostgreSQL\18\bin), SMOKE_ADMIN_URL (scripts/smoke.py only).
